We provide a option to dequantize weights separately here because using fusion kernel is not always better.
For example, W4A16 GEMM kernel fuses dequantization kernels with the matrix multplication kernel to avoid writing dequantized weights into DRAM, but its performance is worse under some specific workloads.
As shown in Table \ref{tab:perf_comp_fusion}, when M is small, the operator is memory-bound. 
The fusion kernel achieves lower latency by reducing global memory accesses.
However, when M is bigger than 4096, performing dequantization and GEMM separately is 12\% to 26\% faster than fusion W4A16 kernels.

One of the mainly reasons is that fusion kernel concurrently performs both dequantization and matrix multiplication, which increases its demand for on-chip hardware resources such as registers and shared memory. 
This increase in resource consumption directly constrain the number of threads that can be active simultaneously on a streaming multiprocessor (SM), leading to reduced thread-level parallelism and lower overall GPU utilization. 
In contrast, separate kernels eliminate the impact of dequantization operations on GEMM, allowing for more efficient resource allocation and higher parallelism under high computational workloads.